## Solution

**Claim:** The rank of the $(2n+1)\times(2n+1)$ matrix $A$ is $\boxed{2n}$.

### Setup and circulant structure

Index rows and columns by $0,1,\dots,2n$ and set $N=2n+1$. Define $g:\{0,1,\dots,2n\}\to\{-1,0,1\}$ by
$$g(0)=0,\qquad g(k)=1\ \text{for }1\le k\le n,\qquad g(k)=-1\ \text{for }n+1\le k\le 2n.$$

From the problem's description, $a_{ij}$ depends only on $i-j$, with $a_{ij}=1$ for $i-j\in\{1,\dots,n\}\cup\{-(2n),\dots,-(n+1)\}$ and $a_{ij}=-1$ for $i-j\in\{n+1,\dots,2n\}\cup\{-1,\dots,-n\}$. Reducing $i-j$ modulo $N$:

| $i-j$ | $\bmod\ N$ | $g$ value |
|---|---|---|
| $1,\dots,n$ | $1,\dots,n$ | $+1$ ✓ |
| $n+1,\dots,2n$ | $n+1,\dots,2n$ | $-1$ ✓ |
| $-1,\dots,-n$ | $2n,\dots,n+1$ | $-1$ ✓ |
| $-(n+1),\dots,-2n$ | $n,\dots,1$ | $+1$ ✓ |

So $a_{ij}=g\bigl((i-j)\bmod N\bigr)$; **$A$ is a circulant matrix**.

### Eigenvalues

The eigenvectors of a circulant are $v_m=(1,\omega^m,\omega^{2m},\dots,\omega^{(N-1)m})^{\top}$, $\omega=e^{2\pi i/N}$, with eigenvalues
$$\lambda_m=\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}g(k)\,\omega^{-mk}=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\omega^{-mk}-\sum_{k=n+1}^{2n}\omega^{-mk},\qquad m=0,1,\dots,2n.$$

**$m=0$:** $\lambda_0=\sum_{k=1}^{n}1-\sum_{k=n+1}^{2n}1=n-n=0$.

**$m\ne 0$:** Re-index the second sum via $k\mapsto n+k$:
$$\sum_{k=n+1}^{2n}\omega^{-mk}=\omega^{-mn}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\omega^{-mk},$$
so
$$\lambda_m=(1-\omega^{-mn})\sum_{k=1}^{n}\omega^{-mk}.$$
The geometric sum (valid since $m\ne0\Rightarrow\omega^{-m}\ne1$) gives $\sum_{k=1}^{n}\omega^{-mk}=\dfrac{\omega^{-m}(1-\omega^{-mn})}{1-\omega^{-m}}$, hence
$$\lambda_m=\frac{\omega^{-m}(1-\omega^{-mn})^{2}}{1-\omega^{-m}}.$$

### When is $\lambda_m=0$?

For $m\ne0$, the denominator $1-\omega^{-m}\ne0$, so $\lambda_m=0\iff 1-\omega^{-mn}=0\iff \omega^{-mn}=1\iff N\mid mn$.

Let $d=\gcd(m,N)$. Then $N\mid mn\iff \dfrac{N}{d}\mid n$. But $\dfrac{N}{d}\mid N$ as well, so $\dfrac{N}{d}\mid\gcd(N,n)=\gcd(2n+1,n)=\gcd(1,n)=1$. Thus $\dfrac{N}{d}=1$, i.e. $N\mid m$, impossible for $m\in\{1,\dots,2n\}$.

**Therefore $\lambda_m\ne0$ for every $m=1,\dots,2n$.**

### Conclusion

$A$ is circulant, hence diagonalizable by the Fourier matrix. Exactly one eigenvalue ($\lambda_0$) vanishes; the remaining $2n$ are nonzero. Thus
$$\operatorname{rank}(A)=2n.$$

(Consistency check: $A$ is skew-symmetric of odd order, so $\det A=0$ and the rank is even and $\le 2n$—matching our result. For $n=1$ the $3\times3$ matrix $\begin{pmatrix}0&-1&1\\1&0&-1\\-1&1&0\end{pmatrix}$ has rank $2=2n$. ✓)

